From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: the refresh tick slows down while it has nothing to do

What: QEMU's display refresh timer (gui_update) runs at the Mac display's
rate for every listener (8 ms on a 120 Hz panel). It now slows to 500 ms
after a second without work for it: a 2D update or a new scanout (boot,
consoles), an update of an extra output's window (those draw on the tick),
a main-window frame not shown yet, or OMACVM_GL_PRESENT_ON_TICK. The next
such update brings the display's rate back at once (QEMU re-arms the timer
when a listener's interval drops). The main window's GL frames do not
count: dpy_gl_update puts them on screen (or the present timer and the
display link do), so a blinking cursor keeps the slow tick.
OMACVM_IDLE_REFRESH=0 keeps the display's rate. GL mode only; QEMU's log
says which.

Why: with virtio-gpu the tick has nothing to do while the screen does not
change (the device's gfx_update does nothing). It only checked the
pasteboard and woke QEMU's main loop 60-120 times a second on an idle
Omarchy desktop, also with the window minimised or hidden.

Upstream: not sent (QEMU's VNC server slows its own refresh the same way;
the Cocoa GL present path and OmacVM's extra outputs are OmacVM patches).
Test: Tests/display/test-idle-refresh.c runs the rate logic, taken from the
patched ui/cocoa.m, in every build. Idle wakeups and CPU of QEMU per process
(proc_pid_rusage) before and after, window shown and minimised (numbers in
the idle-power PR); a notification and a console switch after idle still
show at once. Applies after qemu-cocoa-gl-present-color.patch.
